WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.969
 

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.445
Come close, I've got a secret for you...

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.047
...closer,

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.348
...closer,

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.983
...closer...

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.418
Ok, ready?

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.507
This is 'What If,'

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:44.577
and here's what would happen if
everyone on Earth lived in one city.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:49.482
To date, the greatest population
density ever recorded

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:52.719
was in Hong Kong's
Kowloon Walled City.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.989
Before forced evictions
took place in the early 1990s,

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.092
there were 33,000 people
sharing this space,

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:02.829
which is about the same area
as three football fields.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:08.201
And that's equivalent to 1.2 million
people per square kilometer.

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:11.871
You can understand why
this walled city had to go.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.474
But even if that's an extreme example,

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:17.110
our world is home to
some pretty dense cities.

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:22.882
Manila is a city with roughly
43,000 people per square kilometer.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:27.520
While the population of Manhattan is
about 26,000 per square kilometer.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.824
And while they're not ideal
circumstances for claustrophobics,

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.926
most people can make it work.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.268
It could...

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.572
but comfort would
vary depending on the density.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:49.843
Let's start with a fun city that
has a pretty low population density.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:54.647
New Orleans is roughly
784 people per square kilometer.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:58.718
If you were to scale New Orleans to
accommodate everyone in the world,

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:02.589
you'd get a city the size of
the continental United States.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:07.360
But if you were to do the same thing to
a high population density city like Manila,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.897
which, as we said, has a
population density of 43,000,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:16.970
then everyone in the world would have to fit
into a city the size of the country of Uruguay.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:22.775
It's hard to speculate on what life would be like
if everyone in the world lived in a city this size.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:25.612
In some ways, it might be more sustainable.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:30.350
We'd save money on huge infrastructure
projects like long highways and bridges.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.652
Travel and transport costs would go down,

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:36.623
since all the materials we'd need
would be going to the same place;

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.892
and that might make
basic human necessities

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:41.794
like food and medicine more accessible.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.731
Not to mention, having everything
from work to home

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:47.901
to the supermarket and the
shopping mall within walking distance,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:52.438
or accessible by public transit, would
reduce our environmental impact.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:52.472
or accessible by public transit, would
reduce our environmental impact.
In fact, people in cities
use half as much energy

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:55.942
In fact, people in cities
use half as much energy

00:02:55.942 --> 00:03:00.013
as people who don't – because they
live in smaller spaces.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:04.884
But when we think about urban living today,
it's impossible to ignore the cost of rent.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.520
With space at such a premium,

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.427
And what kind of quality of life
could low-earners expect?

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:17.430
In Hong Kong, for example,
an incredibly dense city

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.033
with the most expensive
housing market in the world,

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:24.971
a 40 square meter (430 sq. ft.)
apartment costs about 700,000 USD.

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:29.209
In some places, that kind of
money could buy you a castle.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:35.181
But with the world crammed into one city,
your living quarters might look more like this.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:39.752
The kind of geo-city we're envisioning
raises a lot of social questions.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.388
People tend to move to cities
for better opportunities,

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:45.091
but, would there be
enough to go around?

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.627
Would the high concentration
of wealth inequality

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.629
that's often overlooked in today's world

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.600
breed more crime? Or even revolution?

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.134
Would there still be wars?

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:59.339
Or would proximity to one another help
us to understand each other a little better,

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:02.976
and realize that we're
not so different after all?

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:05.411
A truly global city is hard to imagine

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.714
because distance and
diverse perspectives

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:11.117
have bred different approaches
to urban planning.

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:14.587
Is there a one-size fits all
approach to cities?

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:18.625
How can we make urban life
sustainable and accommodating to all?

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:22.095
Luckily, the world will never
live in just one city,

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:25.465
but these are questions we
need to start taking seriously.

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.434
Because urban density is on the rise,
and like it or not,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:32.238
we're just gonna have to get
more comfortable with each other.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:34.474
Do you know someone
who's claustrophobic?

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:38.478
Show them this video, and
stay tuned for more 'What If.'

